TITLE:: REJSON
summary:: JSON for the score files: a writer, and a reader that types what parseJSON returns
categories:: Libraries>reCording
related:: Guides/reCording, Classes/REScore

DESCRIPTION::
sclang has no JSON writer, and code::String:parseJSON:: hands every scalar back as a String. REJSON writes nil, Booleans, Integers, Floats (the shortest string that reads back to the same Float, code::.0:: kept on integral ones; a non-finite number as null, with a warning), escaped Strings (UTF-8 through), Symbols as strings with a leading backslash (code::"\\amp"::), Arrays and Lists, Dictionaries and Events (keys as strings); and reads them back to the same classes. A String that would be mistaken for a number, a Boolean or a Symbol is written as code::{"str": "..."}::. Objects read back as IdentityDictionaries with Symbol keys. Output is stable for diffs: keys in a given order first, the rest sorted; pretty-printed down to a depth, compact below.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: stringify
argument:: obj
argument:: indent
Spaces per level; nil (the default) writes everything on one line.
argument:: inlineDepth
Nodes at this depth and deeper are written on one line (code::2:: with the score layout: one event per line).
argument:: keyOrder
Keys (Symbols or Strings) written first, in that order; the others follow sorted.

METHOD:: parse
argument:: text
returns:: The typed value, nil for nil text or a parse error (reported).

METHOD:: write
argument:: obj
argument:: path
Folders are made on the way.
argument:: indent
argument:: inlineDepth
argument:: keyOrder
returns:: The standardized path written.

METHOD:: read
argument:: path
returns:: The typed value, nil (reported) when the file is missing.

METHOD:: mkdirAll
argument:: dir
Makes the folder and its parents.

EXAMPLES::

code::
REJSON.stringify((a: 1, b: [1.5, "x", nil], s: \amp));       // {"a": 1, "b": [1.5, "x", null], "s": "\\amp"}
REJSON.parse("{\"n\": \"42\", \"s\": \"\\\\amp\"}");         // 'n' -> 42 (an Integer), 's' -> amp (a Symbol)
REJSON.stringify("42");                                        // {"str": "42"}: reads back as the String
::
